LanguageData.ProdConsole.Help.Production.List.Body:
Orders are sorted by priority, with High at the top. Orders at the same priority are sorted by product name so the list does not keep rearranging itself.

Search matches product names. The nearby filter narrows the list by state:

— running: the order is active and its bills are assigned to workbenches;
— paused: you stopped the order; its settings are kept, but its bills are removed;
— blocked: the console wants to continue but cannot, and shows the reason;
— completed: the requested amount has been made.

The colored badge on the card and in the list shows the same state. A red badge means the order was not stopped by you, and the reason is always shown beside it.

LanguageData.ProdConsole.Help.Production.Blocked.Body:
The reason is written directly on the order card and on the Overview page:

— not enough resources: the allowed source storages do not contain the required materials;
— no workbench: no suitable workbench exists, or every suitable bench is broken, unpowered, or reserved by another console order;
— nobody to work: none of the selected workers can perform the job or has that work type enabled;
— not researched: the recipe has not been unlocked;
— source or destination storage is gone: the order still points to a storage that was removed;
— workbenches, workers, or storages are disabled: their Use checkbox was cleared on the relevant page.

The order and all of its settings remain intact. Fix the cause and it will resume automatically.

LanguageData.ProdConsole.Help.Production.What.Body:
The What to make panel contains the product, mode, and amount.

— fixed amount: make N items, then finish;
— maintain stock: keep N items in storage and make more whenever stock falls below the target;
— forever: keep working until you stop the order;
— number of runs: used for operations without a finished product, such as butchering, smelting, or burning.

This panel also lets you choose a material when the recipe allows one and set the permitted skill range. RimWorld applies that range directly, so a colonist outside it will not take the bill.

LanguageData.ProdConsole.Help.Production.From.Body:
Materials controls what the order is allowed to consume. Clearing an option means "do not use this"—useful when your wood is meant for construction rather than fuel.

Sources controls where materials may be collected:

— all available: anywhere on the map;
— selected storages: only the ones you choose;
— radius: only items within that many cells of the workbench.

A radius is useful for a remote workshop, so a colonist will not cross the whole map for one log. If the area is empty, however, the order will report not enough resources even when the material exists elsewhere.

LanguageData.ProdConsole.Help.Production.Where.Body:
Workbenches lists the benches allowed to take this order. One workbench can serve several orders: their bills wait in its queue by priority, and if the top order is missing materials, the bench moves on to the next one.

Several workbenches contribute to one shared total. Five benches assigned to an order for one hundred items will make one hundred together, not one hundred each.

Destination decides what happens to finished goods:

— any suitable storage: normal RimWorld behavior — the crafter hauls the product wherever your storage settings send it;
— chosen storage: the product is taken there, and only there;
— drop on floor: the crafter drops the product next to the bench and starts the next one right away, while other colonists haul it to storage.

LanguageData.ProdConsole.Help.Wizard.Sources.Body:
This step decides where materials may be collected:

— all available: the whole map;
— selected storages: only checked storages;
— radius: an area around the workbench.

The total at the bottom shows how much usable material lies inside the selected area. A zero here is a reliable sign that the order will be blocked as soon as it is created.

LanguageData.ProdConsole.Help.Growing.Sowing.Body:
The checkbox in the section header hands the field over to the console. You can then choose a mode:

— Repeat: keep growing the same crop after every harvest;
— Queue: grow crops from a list, one after another;
— Do not sow: keep the field managed but assign no crop.

Repeat only offers crops that can be sown right now. The list is shorter in winter because selecting a crop that cannot grow would leave the field silently stuck.

Cut the current crop is for cases where you cannot wait for harvest. The existing plants are cut down and the selected crop is sown immediately.

LanguageData.ProdConsole.Help.Production.Footer.Body:
At the bottom of the order card:

— pause and resume: the order's bills are removed from the workbenches, and its settings are kept;
— use as template: opens the new order wizard with this order's settings — workers, storage, workbenches, mode, and amount. All that is left is to pick the product;
— save: applies your changes; the button stays dimmed while there is nothing to save;
— delete: the console asks first, because a deleted order cannot be restored. Its bills are removed from the workbenches, your own bills that the console set aside are put back, and anything already made stays in storage.

LanguageData.ProdConsole.Help.Wizard.Pack.Body:
If you tick several products on the first step, the wizard builds a batch: the shared settings are entered once, and you get a separate order for each product.

— amount: each product has its own mode and amount;
— materials: identical materials used by several products are shown as one group, labeled with what they are for;
— workbenches: each workbench shows which products in the batch it can make; every product needs at least one selected workbench;
— workers and storage: shared by all orders in the batch; the destination storage must accept every product.

Once created, these are ordinary orders: you edit, pause, and delete them one at a time. Combined products such as meat and operations such as butchering cannot join a batch — they are created on their own.

You can also create similar orders one by one: the Use as template button on an order card opens the wizard with that order's settings.
